{:db/id 17592186124455 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186124259 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "f6400ab7-3684-5f0d-b8f0-1c85b0b322ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-3363" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x^2-3xy+4y=2.$ Find a tangent vector to the curve at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, if we set $f(x,y) = x^2-3xy+4y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(1,1) = f_y(1,1) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(1,1) \\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3xy+4y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(-3x+4)\\text{ }i-(2x-3y)\\text{ }j \\\\ t(1,1) & =(-3(1)+4)\\text{ }i-(2(1)-3(1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =1\\,i-(-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $\\mathbf i +\\mathbf j,$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/01/0114bf524d085402bd27178d687a5faf176b7147c8e8726b84042b17823b6b0b.png)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186124980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3021435f-4fc5-5325-909c-5c4aa929c4c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i-j" :db/id 17592186458723} {:answer/id #uuid "a73148e2-c414-5427-b687-2d5af8547b72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i" :db/id 17592186458927} {:answer/id #uuid "b0875f94-b02d-58ee-876a-7de7153c2d0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id 17592186458945} {:answer/id #uuid "c99e77dc-e78f-5088-831c-2eddf2080e1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id 17592186458985} {:answer/id #uuid "fec8b574-6fb0-5eaa-9cb4-748309ad20fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-6\\,j" :db/id 17592186459081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fec8b574-6fb0-5eaa-9cb4-748309ad20fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-6\\,j" :db/id 17592186459081} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea33b86b-0147-5727-9ec2-461d7ee2b6cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186122480}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d538882c-b1cd-5192-bbe8-86de583ea5af" :question/math-academy-id "q-36391" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}y=xy+4.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =xy+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-xy & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(2,2)={f}_{y}(2,2)\\,i-{f}_{x}(2,2)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-xy)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-xy)\\,j \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-x)\\,i-(2xy-y)\\,j \\\\ t(2,2) & =({2}^{2}-2)\\,i-(2(2)(2)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-6\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\,i-6\\,j.$"} {:db/id 17592186125191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "323ab6d8-7707-5cf4-a618-ba17b3923dd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j" :db/id 17592186364297} {:answer/id #uuid "043f0c6b-beee-5929-91a6-329b0aba6cab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j" :db/id 17592186364298} {:answer/id #uuid "6c7f3a73-6bb3-57e1-b890-8a39b32ac5a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+j" :db/id 17592186364299} {:answer/id #uuid "b9bd5e2f-eea5-58c3-8f18-2c41ef884348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i-j" :db/id 17592186364300} {:answer/id #uuid "cb787b13-c2e5-5275-b510-4a2ee9675a16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i-j" :db/id 17592186364301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "323ab6d8-7707-5cf4-a618-ba17b3923dd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j" :db/id 17592186364297} :answer-field/id #uuid "4633732e-7712-5dd5-9f2e-d57d7262b765" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186364296}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ad74cdc7-0885-5bf3-989d-0d3835ead91a" :question/math-academy-id "q-65204" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})={f}_{y}(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})i-{f}_{x}(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\ t(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}))i-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}))j \\\\  & =-i+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-i+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j.$"} {:db/id 17592186265852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab7ecfb-6837-5f43-a7dc-5de26787b651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id 17592186263102} {:answer/id #uuid "23c6bb01-4817-5934-b31c-f94122a313f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i+j" :db/id 17592186263311} {:answer/id #uuid "4d97af51-08fe-5c7f-90df-c14d7845e35f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+j" :db/id 17592186263672} {:answer/id #uuid "96b21983-40a1-5c92-8a77-84386b07a32d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j" :db/id 17592186264290} {:answer/id #uuid "eae56032-740f-556c-b1d0-0bf3c280086c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j" :db/id 17592186265047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ab7ecfb-6837-5f43-a7dc-5de26787b651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id 17592186263102} :answer-field/id #uuid "86808a14-0099-527e-bba9-8dfc049cb102" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e97b736-408e-55e1-a895-d40d15156508" :question/math-academy-id "q-130198" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)={f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)i-{f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)j \\\\  & =(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)j \\\\ t(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))j \\\\  & =i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $i-j.$"} {:db/id 17592186265853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db8855f-407d-543c-b41c-01ec8863c250" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+{e}^{2}\\,j" :db/id 17592186263273} {:answer/id #uuid "310e7ac2-6457-50a9-b85b-62fe72227f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-e\\,j" :db/id 17592186263418} {:answer/id #uuid "313c22eb-142a-5e0a-bbf7-3e694214c7e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i" :db/id 17592186263420} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef9f166-57a7-581f-b069-feefd3a089a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id 17592186263557} {:answer/id #uuid "66d4bf27-1916-5b14-ad54-71713514a6d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e\\,i+j" :db/id 17592186263890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "310e7ac2-6457-50a9-b85b-62fe72227f50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-e\\,j" :db/id 17592186263418} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0fb381c-a4a1-50c1-8a16-52f23963275a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186265583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b37d7050-f209-536a-923e-f6a34554696d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130200" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}=e-y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1} & =e-y \\\\ x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=e.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,0)={f}_{y}(1,0)i-{f}_{x}(1,0)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y)j \\\\  & =(2xy{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+1)i-{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\ t(1,0) & =(2(1)(0){e}^{0+1}+1)i-{e}^{0+1}\\,j \\\\  & =i-e\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $i-e\\,j.$"} {:db/id 17592186323918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18cbb66c-c87e-532e-b215-79cebdd50220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2e-8)\\,i+(\\frac{e}{2}-8)j" :db/id 17592186321343} {:answer/id #uuid "25737ab8-5934-517a-82e3-355f226249ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-4e)\\,i+(\\frac{2}{e}-8)j" :db/id 17592186321438} {:answer/id #uuid "569fd915-b891-5273-a763-b23c030c775d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2-8e)\\,i+(2-8e)j" :db/id 17592186321831} {:answer/id #uuid "91bfacf0-e81a-591f-b5c7-eb843413fffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)\\,j" :db/id 17592186322304} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c24a19-a0b8-5781-9f03-f3684c7d7f7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8-2e)\\,i+(\\frac{8}{e}-2)j" :db/id 17592186322954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91bfacf0-e81a-591f-b5c7-eb843413fffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)\\,j" :db/id 17592186322304} :answer-field/id #uuid "2328f82b-ecf4-5e93-897c-028b4fd16484" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186323237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12b276e2-b703-55f7-94b8-069326b3f510" :question/math-academy-id "q-37516" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=8xy+2-8e.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,e).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =8xy+2-8e \\\\ 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy & =2-8e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=2-8e.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,e)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,e)={f}_{y}(1,e)\\,i-{f}_{x}(1,e)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,e):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x}{y}-8x)i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-8y)\\,j \\\\ t(1,e) & =(\\frac{2(1)}{e}-8(1))i-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-8(e))\\,j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $(\\frac{2}{e}-8)i+(8e-2)\\,j.$"} {:db/id 17592186339107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a7f7daf-eab5-5f09-9994-fb78510d1452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i+6\\,j" :db/id 17592186336857} {:answer/id #uuid "5bdf6205-616a-5fe8-bae1-1eafc7069834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id 17592186336867} {:answer/id #uuid "5f640f70-dd55-53af-a444-45f3d1013ded" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-j" :db/id 17592186336897} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1b9f33-c38c-59c8-99f2-2467570782ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+6\\,j" :db/id 17592186337000} {:answer/id #uuid "9f2d1ea8-f373-5f19-800d-34e70ba82c85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-6\\,j" :db/id 17592186337470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a7f7daf-eab5-5f09-9994-fb78510d1452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i+6\\,j" :db/id 17592186336857} :answer-field/id #uuid "31c46a4e-d03a-5a5a-b5e5-3a014deba440" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "148b9435-dbdc-5e2a-8580-1b7906c6cec6" :question/math-academy-id "q-72400" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2xy+9.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2xy+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,2)={f}_{y}(-1,2)\\,i-{f}_{x}(-1,2)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2xy-9)\\,j \\\\  & =(2y-2x)\\,i-(2x-2y)\\,j \\\\ t(-1,2) & =(2(2)-2(-1))\\,i-(2(-1)-2(2))\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i+6\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $6\\,i+6\\,j.$"} {:db/id 17592186339122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a24f4b8-a184-5828-8c72-dcc5b94e5943" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,i-5\\,j" :db/id 17592186336423} {:answer/id #uuid "2afed586-1f6a-50fd-ad21-c54f17e514f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i" :db/id 17592186336436} {:answer/id #uuid "af297190-71a7-5741-8317-95edf63e83e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+5\\,j" :db/id 17592186337604} {:answer/id #uuid "be71b2b4-857d-5122-ac95-bc07fb632330" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,i-j" :db/id 17592186337725} {:answer/id #uuid "c71d302c-3c74-5be4-b48a-d3f52171a59d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,j" :db/id 17592186337799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c71d302c-3c74-5be4-b48a-d3f52171a59d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,j" :db/id 17592186337799} :answer-field/id #uuid "26dc6ba3-c35c-5f43-8491-310a9b2258ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186338352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c4a11cf-62cb-5e11-869c-4992e2a1b6f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-72463" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy=1-3x.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy & =1-3x \\\\ 2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,1)={f}_{y}(1,1)i-{f}_{x}(1,1)j\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-4xy+3x)j \\\\  & =(4y{x}^{3}-4x)i-(6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-4y+3)j \\\\ t(1,1) & =(4(1)(1)^{3}-4(1))i-(6(1)^{2}(1)^{2}-4(1)+3)j \\\\  & =-5\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-5\\,j.$"} {:db/id 17592186363031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f3795a-9c5d-564b-99c5-8234a8f8adae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,j" :db/id 17592186363033} {:answer/id #uuid "17117ea5-c48d-5a75-87dd-44fd4b306516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-2\\,j" :db/id 17592186363034} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a1d040-84f6-5093-b8b7-a02c512a3ef2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id 17592186363035} {:answer/id #uuid "afb47ff5-03cb-5158-9e15-6ff6eeca5436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i" :db/id 17592186363036} {:answer/id #uuid "0c788786-b66b-5944-b4ad-21c56064f8a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+3\\,j" :db/id 17592186363037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c788786-b66b-5944-b4ad-21c56064f8a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+3\\,j" :db/id 17592186363037} :answer-field/id #uuid "d07c4d10-4f15-5f81-9aca-9c05aa1a4821" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186363032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55fb49e6-3301-5747-997a-6aea4f919a61" :question/math-academy-id "q-130201" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $xy=3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & =3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{3} \\\\ xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=3.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,-1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,-1)={f}_{y}(-1,-1)i-{f}_{x}(-1,-1)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3})j \\\\  & =(x+3{y}^{2})i-(y+2x)j \\\\ t(-1,-1) & =(-1+3(-1)^{2})i-(-1+2(-1))j \\\\  & =2\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\,i+3\\,j.$"} {:db/id 17592186364309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b63d502-5d59-57aa-8402-34f229d7c402" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id 17592186364311} {:answer/id #uuid "d929314d-5095-5284-a04d-ed11a462fa9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-3\\,j" :db/id 17592186364312} {:answer/id #uuid "47b22ede-a70a-5c2c-93d8-02196b170ae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id 17592186364313} {:answer/id #uuid "18da3830-6440-532f-9cfa-3f5d7f38caec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+j" :db/id 17592186364314} {:answer/id #uuid "41a6476e-7908-5df0-9348-8c27d17118c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+3\\,j" :db/id 17592186364315}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d929314d-5095-5284-a04d-ed11a462fa9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-3\\,j" :db/id 17592186364312} :answer-field/id #uuid "727e4de1-593d-5c99-aa03-6535fb642416" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186364310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ddd3bf49-0b80-5ad0-bb5a-ddf4f0c0659f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72388" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x+1={x}^{2}y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={x}^{2}y \\\\ {x}^{2}y-x-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-x-1$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,2)={f}_{y}(1,2)i-{f}_{x}(1,2)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-x-1)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-x-1)j \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,i-(2xy-1)j \\\\ t(1,2) & =(1)^{2}\\,i-(2(1)(2)-1)j \\\\  & =i-3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $i-3\\,j.$"} {:db/id 17592186373743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b36ad97d-7477-5e7d-8ba7-295eb688f029" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,j" :db/id 17592186373745} {:answer/id #uuid "118ee41d-f5a0-5dff-b95c-7ca94836bc57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,i" :db/id 17592186373746} {:answer/id #uuid "19b66868-06fe-5184-957b-7342b00b0910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-4\\,j" :db/id 17592186373747} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3d37cc-037a-5d75-8a4d-47c54303cb93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-j" :db/id 17592186373748} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff73071-c5ea-582c-baaf-95b7a73e5652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i+2\\,j" :db/id 17592186373749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19b66868-06fe-5184-957b-7342b00b0910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-4\\,j" :db/id 17592186373747} :answer-field/id #uuid "8983d5c5-c4ae-595e-a0a2-63b53b664593" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186373744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "591300c7-13a2-5dd3-a27a-1d6a5e36e2a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-130192" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}y={y}^{2}+3.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & ={y}^{2}+3 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-{y}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-{y}^{2},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=3.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(2,1)={f}_{y}(2,1)\\,i-{f}_{x}(2,1)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-{y}^{2})\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2y)\\,i-2xy\\,j \\\\ t(2,1) & =({2}^{2}-2(1))\\,i-2(2)(1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\,i-4\\,j.$"} {:db/id 17592186373750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "911d7e42-cfcf-516f-9283-caa49f997f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i" :db/id 17592186373752} {:answer/id #uuid "0e521a0c-e2f8-593e-ac64-9ff0e485a18b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+j" :db/id 17592186373753} {:answer/id #uuid "35594e4f-af9b-5a65-8a40-4f8e1ea611be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i-2\\,j" :db/id 17592186373754} {:answer/id #uuid "2593f5c0-8a74-5dbd-9a7a-90d0d2eee839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "j" :db/id 17592186373755} {:answer/id #uuid "99b82f0f-6ffa-5c51-818b-49987fd97f69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2i+j" :db/id 17592186373756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35594e4f-af9b-5a65-8a40-4f8e1ea611be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i-2\\,j" :db/id 17592186373754} :answer-field/id #uuid "e43ec9cc-2584-517e-838c-48a27daf6cd8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186373751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8f7db75e-f1c4-5d13-9d48-0ee7ceacaa16" :question/math-academy-id "q-130194" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y={x}^{2}-1.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-{x}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-{x}^{2},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,1)={f}_{y}(-1,1)\\,i-{f}_{x}(-1,1)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-{x}^{2})\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-{x}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{y}\\,i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-2x)\\,j \\\\ t(-1,1) & =\\frac{(-1)}{1}\\,i-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-2(-1))\\,j \\\\  & =-i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-i-2\\,j.$"} {:db/id 17592186447372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9548efa7-b337-5098-83fd-68445ff80070" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-3\\,j" :db/id 17592186447341} {:answer/id #uuid "c60ad1d3-95a7-5a9f-b2b9-64da4df122a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id 17592186447348} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e79c61-6c42-5702-a833-be284f78ce16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,i+5\\,j" :db/id 17592186447358} {:answer/id #uuid "f692be5a-8425-517e-a612-23b54fc9103d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\,i-4\\,j" :db/id 17592186447361} {:answer/id #uuid "f8735719-d369-5e0e-a1c4-c3a71df5f0ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,i-5\\,j" :db/id 17592186447363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c60ad1d3-95a7-5a9f-b2b9-64da4df122a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id 17592186447348} :answer-field/id #uuid "74d8ed8e-549b-58fd-a410-9d0b085953e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186447369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43788191-a65e-5058-bbfd-37ff934a1aa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-72403" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $(x-y)^{2}=2x-y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-y)^{2} & =2x-y \\\\ (x-y)^{2}-2x+y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=(x-y)^{2}-2x+y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(2,0)={f}_{y}(2,0)i-{f}_{x}(2,0)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)^{2}-2x+y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)^{2}-2x+y)j \\\\  & =(-2(x-y)+1)i-(2(x-y)-2)j \\\\ t(2,0) & =(-2((2)-(0))+1)\\,i-((2((2)-(0))-2)\\,j \\\\  & =-3\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-3\\,i-2\\,j.$"} {:db/id 17592186482608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "644861a7-1cbc-5867-8185-defe9888db93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-j" :db/id 17592186482005} {:answer/id #uuid "73996ca2-8d53-576d-9599-e7d064a54b16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,j" :db/id 17592186482042} {:answer/id #uuid "8054282c-e26d-5511-a066-a463f3020bfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id 17592186482072} {:answer/id #uuid "b5877c60-a794-5905-b476-5b03a5663061" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i" :db/id 17592186482203} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a04f0f-fb4c-5c47-a171-0ccaf2eedb86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+2\\,j" :db/id 17592186482358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8054282c-e26d-5511-a066-a463f3020bfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id 17592186482072} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ad3bafa-b0de-589d-9a11-64d4563a5603" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186482443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eaaccc28-17cb-56b1-b96e-6187ebe91480" :question/math-academy-id "q-130203" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${y}^{2}=1-{x}^{2}+2y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =1-{x}^{2}+2y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,2)={f}_{y}(-1,2)i-{f}_{x}(-1,2)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y)j \\\\  & =(2y-2)i-2x\\,j \\\\ t(-1,2) & =(2(2)-2)i-2(-1)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\,i+2\\,j.$"} {:db/id 17592186499529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39ba8864-9639-569c-861f-6e638330546b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id 17592186498857} {:answer/id #uuid "7b5ff22c-2612-54bb-bf13-52b4667b899d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i-j" :db/id 17592186499006} {:answer/id #uuid "9d918342-1efd-5f5f-a70c-62d3002a283b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i" :db/id 17592186499065} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fcce98-7907-5c3f-8f29-c741eb4b7cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i+j" :db/id 17592186499135} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1550ba-8b0e-51be-878a-b2b84082b1f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id 17592186499172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca1550ba-8b0e-51be-878a-b2b84082b1f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-j" :db/id 17592186499172} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7015fd4-ab37-5009-8ea6-02df343db6e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186499428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b96af04a-99a9-59d6-b4b4-fac54542ff08" :question/math-academy-id "q-65205" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x{e}^{y}-1=xy.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}-1 & =xy \\\\ x{e}^{y}-xy & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}-xy$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,0)={f}_{y}(1,0)i-{f}_{x}(1,0)j\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}-xy)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}-xy)j \\\\  & =(x{e}^{y}-x)i-({e}^{y}-y)j \\\\ t(1,0) & =((1){e}^{0}-(1))i-({e}^{0}-(0))j \\\\  & =-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-j.$"} {:db/id 17592186499554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "348fa49f-2af8-5562-91f6-3f70bb93ea90" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e1052d2-3a28-5ca0-9e37-9f2ccf48871c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i-j" :db/id 17592186498760} {:answer/id #uuid "0f687a71-756e-59f6-bc52-a42d2a1ff16b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i-3\\,j" :db/id 17592186498761} {:answer/id #uuid "1ac23b03-c1ad-5ab7-be63-a35fc34705f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-3\\,j" :db/id 17592186498787} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf883f8-d823-5d14-9a1b-8d3c64093c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id 17592186498942} {:answer/id #uuid "fac357f6-d241-5f53-aa60-7685a0cfadeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,i-j" :db/id 17592186499292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e1052d2-3a28-5ca0-9e37-9f2ccf48871c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-i-j" :db/id 17592186498760} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f7da737-6ab6-5dc3-a356-5e38af59041a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186499319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea587ab0-d077-5468-b262-1697c88c7094" :question/math-academy-id "q-72389" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2{e}^{xy}=x+y+1.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{xy} & =x+y+1 \\\\ 2{e}^{xy}-x-y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{e}^{xy}-x-y-1$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(0,1)={f}_{y}(0,1)\\,i-{f}_{x}(0,1)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{e}^{xy}-x-y-1)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{e}^{xy}-x-y-1)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x{e}^{xy}-1)\\,i-(2y{e}^{xy}-1)\\,j \\\\ t(0,1) & =(2(0){e}^{(0)(1)}-1)\\,i-(2(1){e}^{(0)(1)}-1)\\,j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $-i-j.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}
